Read the actual ml-dsa Plantard NTT integration (smallpoly.c, sign.c)
to answer the open question from the previous commit. Findings now
recorded in PLANTARD_NOTES.md:

  - ml-dsa's Plantard NTT does NOT produce mid-NTT byte-identical
    output to a canonical reference. There's NO 'reduce' / 'caddq'
    step between small_ntt and the pointwise helpers. By contrast,
    the regular (non-Plantard) ml-dsa NTT IS followed by explicit
    reduce/caddq.

  - The architecture is: canonical -> small_ntt -> [Plantard rep]
    -> small_point_mul (Plantard-aware) -> small_asymmetric_mul
    (Plantard-aware) -> small_invntt_tomont -> canonical. Byte
    equivalence is at the pipeline boundary, not per-NTT.

  - The asm butterfly uses uadd16 / usub16 — UNSIGNED packed 16-bit
    add/sub that wraps mod 2^16 per half. So the running state is
    'uint16 modulo 2^16, congruent to true value mod Q', NOT centred
    signed int16.

Root cause of my earlier Path A failure: I cast cells to int16_t
(sign-extending bit-15-set cells), then used signed int32 arithmetic.
That's a representation mismatch with the asm's uint16 wrap semantics.
The single-coefficient plant_red was correct, and n=4 happened to
work because no cell had bit 15 set; n>=256 diverged because some
post-butterfly values landed in cells with bit 15 set and were then
sign-extended (instead of left as 'value mod 2^16') by the next
layer's read.

Notes file now lays out the corrected next iteration:
  4. Rewrite plant_18433.c with uint16-wrap arithmetic mirroring
     the asm semantics (not the reference NTT line-for-line).
  5. Write Plantard-aware pointwise helpers (montymul_plant,
     sub_plant, tomonty_plant, plus composed compose_plant for the
     tomonty(sub(montymul, ...)) chain in hawk_sign.c).
  6. Cross-check at the pipeline boundary instead of per-NTT.
  7. Adapt ml-dsa's smallntt_769.S to Q=18433 + n=512/1024.
  8. Benchmark.

This commit is documentation-only; plant_18433.c remains at the
byte-identical baseline (Path B structure) for now.
